    "q-164304": {
      "dom_id": "step-q164304",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}-(z+2)^{2}=-1,$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$",
      "worked_solution": "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}-(z+2)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}+1 & =(z+2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+2)^{2} & =\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}+1} \\\\ z & =-2\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root is defined only for non-negative arguments. However, notice that for all $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}+1\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $z(x,y)$ is ${R}^{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
