    "q-36388": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36388",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet  $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{3}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$",
      "worked_solution": "From the hyperboloid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{3} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-1 & =\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{3} \\\\ (z+1)^{2} & =3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-1) \\\\ (z+1) & =\\pm \\sqrt{3(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-1 & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the exterior of the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1$, including its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\ge 1\\}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
